BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 748.0 Td (      # The Tale of the Spring and the River) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 738.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 728.0 Td (      In ancient times, two villages stood separated by a barren valley. The Mountain Village po) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 718.0 Td (        ssessed a sacred spring that flowed with sweet water, while the Valley Village held fert) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 708.0 Td (        ile soil that could feed multitudes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 698.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 688.0 Td (      Each season, the Mountain folk faced a choice: pour their precious water down into the val) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 678.0 Td (        ley below, trusting it would somehow return to them, or keep it for themselves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 668.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 658.0 Td (      One brave elder named Kiva decided to pour half the spring's bounty into the valley. As th) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 648.0 Td (        e water descended, it multiplied threefold, transforming the parched earth into blooming) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 638.0 Td (         gardens. The Valley people, grateful and wise, saw that Kiva's gift had created abundan) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 628.0 Td (        ce beyond imagination.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 618.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 608.0 Td (      They sent back grain, fruits, and seeds-not merely equal to what they received, but genero) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 598.0 Td (        us enough to honor the trust placed in them. The Mountain Village flourished.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 588.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 578.0 Td (      When the villages grew selfish and withheld from each other, both suffered. But when they ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 568.0 Td (        remembered Kiva's lesson-that water shared becomes a river, and rivers nourish all-prosp) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 558.0 Td (        erity flowed between them like an eternal cycle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 548.0 Td (      ) Tj ET
